A Node image that carries compilers, dev dependencies and source maps into production is slow to pull and has a large attack surface. Multi-stage builds fix both.

## The Dockerfile

```dockerfile {% title="Dockerfile" highlight="10-12" %}
# syntax=docker/dockerfile:1
FROM node:22-alpine AS build
WORKDIR /app
RUN corepack enable
COPY package.json pnpm-lock.yaml ./
RUN pnpm install --frozen-lockfile
COPY . .
RUN pnpm run build && pnpm prune --prod

FROM node:22-alpine
WORKDIR /app
COPY --from=build /app/node_modules ./node_modules
COPY --from=build /app/dist ./dist
USER node
CMD ["node", "dist/main.js"]
```

{% callout type="warning" title="Native modules" %}
Packages such as `bcrypt` or `sqlite3` compile against the build stage's libc. Keep both stages on the same base image (here `alpine`), or they will fail to load at runtime.
{% /callout %}

## Checking the result

```bash
docker build -t api .
docker image ls api --format '{{.Repository}} {{.Size}}'
# api 141MB
```

{% linkcard url="https://docs.docker.com/build/building/multi-stage/" title="Docker docs: Multi-stage builds" /%}
